  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday) \nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nHigh pressure begins to retreat westward starting Tuesday, which\nopens the door for a few shortwaves to slide down the eastern \nperiphery of the ridge through the region for the second half of \nthe forecast period. Latest model solutions indicate the first of \nthese waves and associated weak frontal boundary approaching the \nCWA Tuesday. Ahead of the boundary, continued hot and humid \nconditions continue with the potential for pooling moisture \nalong/near the Oklahoma Kansas border as the boundary approaches. \nThus, additional heat headlines are likely again Tuesday \nafternoon. \n\nThe boundary is forecast to move into the region Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday and potentially set up from northwest to southeast\nover northeast Oklahoma into western Arkansas. Slight to low end \nchances for showers/storms develop mainly along and east of this \nboundary, closer to the weak low pressure center. This activity \nlooks to exit Wednesday night.\n\nTiming differences/strength of the second shortwave shows up in\nthe data Thursday/Friday. However, the boundary or some form of\nthe boundary from Wednesday looks to remain over the region which\ncould spark another slight to low end chance for showers/storms\nagain for the eastern half of the CWA Thursday afternoon and\nFriday. Details with the rain chances and the boundary location\nwill determine the degree to any cooler conditions for the CWA \nlate week. \n\nIn the extended forecast period, model solutions continue to  \nhighlight a more defined wave and cold front trying to push\nsoutheast through the Plains next weekend. Precip chances extend\ninto Saturday for parts of the region, while conditions more\ncloser to seasonal average develop for the CWA for next weekend.\nTiming and details will continue to be revamped through the week,\nbut there is potential for a break in the current hot/humid\nconditions.",
